{
  "title": "Normalize: house-style op that rewrites recipes to the owner's preferences (weights, explicit prep steps, ingredient order, a style guide)",
  "description": "A new normalize op (add it to NormalizeOp in ramekin-core/src/pipeline/normalize_ops.rs, which landed in normalize-1) that uses a frontier model to rewrite a recipe into the owner's house style. \
Nothing in the repo records these preferences yet (no prompt or doc mentions weights or prep steps), so the first deliverable is a written style spec checked in (e.g. doc/house-style.md) that the prompt embeds and an ai-eval suite judges against. \
Known preferences: (1) Weight-based measures by default: grams first, using the ingredient catalog's densities and piece weights (the same data calorie estimates use) rather than the model's guesses wherever the catalog can weigh the line, keeping the original volume or count alongside when useful (e.g. '120 g (1 cup) flour') and leaving things like '1 egg' or 'salt to taste' as they are. (2) No implicit prep in ingredient lines: '1 onion, chopped' becomes '1 onion' plus an explicit 'Chop the onion' step at the point it's needed. (3) Ingredients listed in the order they are used, or grouped into sections (e.g. 'For the sauce', 'For the dough') when the recipe has distinct components, with each section in order of use; the op uses the existing ingredient `section` field rather than inventing headings in item text. (4) A consistent style guide. \
Options for (4), for the owner to pick: NYT Cooking (ingredients in order of use, imperative steps, specific doneness cues; note it keeps prep in the ingredient line, which conflicts with (2)); Serious Eats (very explicit steps with reasons, weights common); King Arthur Baking (weight-first, very consistent units); America's Test Kitchen / Cook's Illustrated (prep in the ingredient list, one action per step); or our own short spec borrowing from these. \
Recommendation: our own spec, NYT-style voice and ingredient ordering plus weights first and prep moved into steps. \
Also decide: sentence case vs title case for ingredient names, unit abbreviations (g, tbsp), temperature format (°F with °C?), and whether steps are split one action each. \
The op should keep ingredient structure valid for the parser and calorie estimates (the rewritten lines must still parse; the PR should show before/after on pipeline fixtures). \
Build an ai-eval suite (data/ai-evals/) with a handful of golden before/after recipes and a judge prompt keyed to the spec. \
Owner decisions (2026-10-25): our own spec (NYT-style voice and ingredient ordering, weights first, prep moved into steps); grams first with the original volume or count in parentheses when useful ('120 g (1 cup) flour'), short unit abbreviations (g, tbsp, tsp); when the catalog can't weigh a line, leave it as-is and never use the model's guessed weights; lowercase ingredient names except proper nouns ('Dijon mustard'); temperatures in °F only ('425°F'); steps as short related groups, one coherent stage each, not strictly one action per step; brief whys for non-obvious steps (Serious Eats style), with no added doneness cues or timing rewrites; the op runs by default on import like the other AI normalize ops (`make pipeline` runs no AI, so pipeline data won't change).",
